  "finalText": "Here's a 12-day Everest Base Camp itinerary for October — the best trekking window of the year with stable skies and clear mountain views. I've built in two dedicated acclimatisation days (Namche and Dingboche) because altitude sickness is the number one reason people abandon this trek; going slower is genuinely faster here. October teahouse availability is tight, so book Namche and Gorak Shep nights ahead through your operator.\n\nKey risks to respect: AMS above 4,000 m (know the symptoms, descend if they worsen), the Lukla flight's weather delays (keep a buffer day before your international flight), and cold nights above Lobuche — pack a four-season sleeping bag. A licensed guide is now required for foreign trekkers in the Khumbu, and rescue insurance covering helicopter evacuation above 5,000 m is essential.\n\nEstimated cost: £2,450 per person, including the Kathmandu–Lukla flights (£320), Sagarmatha permits, a licensed guide and porter, teahouse accommodation and meals. Your £3,000 budget covers this comfortably with a contingency for a rest-day bakery habit in Namche Bazaar.\n\nSay the word and I'll search flights from your home airport to Kathmandu, or adjust the pacing — this plan suits someone running a couple of times a week."
